Return -1 from opj_stream_get_number_byte_left() instead of failing an assert when the stream
position is past the declared stream length, and treat a negative result as end of stream (or
an error) in its callers.

opj_j2k_read_tile_header() seeks to tile-part offsets that opj_j2k_build_tp_index_from_tlm()
derives from the TLM marker lengths without checking them against the stream length. The
in-memory callbacks of tests/fuzzers/opj_decompress_fuzzer_{J2K,JP2}.cpp store the position in a
size_t, so with a 32-bit size_t (wasm32 here) a seek to 2^32 + n lands at n and succeeds. The
reads that follow return data while m_byte_offset is beyond m_user_data_length, and the next
opj_stream_get_number_byte_left() call (in opj_j2k_read_tile_header(), or in
opj_jp2_read_boxhdr() for a last box of length 0) aborts on its assert. On 64-bit hosts the
fuzzer's read callback fails at that offset and decoding stops cleanly. Includes
test_tlm_seek_past_end, which builds such a codestream and decodes it through callbacks that
keep 32-bit positions; it aborts without this change. Rejecting seeks beyond
m_user_data_length in opj_stream_read_seek(), or beyond nLength in the fuzzers' SeekCallback,
would also avoid the abort.
Not fixed upstream as of 8314119 (master on 2026-11-21).
Upstream: none found (uclouvain/openjpeg issues and PRs searched for
opj_stream_get_number_byte_left on 2026-11-21; #1151 and #455 are older reports of the same
assert on other paths).

